FINDINGS:

1. [BLOCKER] src/lsp.rs:1288 — Standard-module canonicalisation resolves an import alias through the flat, name-keyed `module_aliases` map instead of the receiver's own binding, so two imports that reuse an alias in different scopes corrupt each other. `import files as f` at top level plus `import json as f` inside a function/branch leaves `module_aliases["f"] == "json"` (`src/lsp.rs:1940` unconditionally inserts by spelling); the top-level `f.read("x")` then canonicalises to `json`, `module_has_member("json","read")` is false, and goto/references answer nothing (and `f.parse` can falsely resolve to `json.parse`). This contradicts the delta's claim that "an import alias canonicalises to the module it named". Fix: record alias→source per import binding (extend `import_module_bindings` to also carry stdlib sources, or key the alias map by binding) and have `standard_module_of` use that instead of the name-keyed table; add a test with the same alias imported as two different modules in two scopes. VERIFIED: traced `src/lsp.rs:1940` (`module_aliases.insert(name.to_string(), source)`) and `src/lsp.rs:1288-1292` (`.get(receiver_name)`), with the receiver binding (`DefinitionKind::Import`) never mapped to its actual source.

2. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:1298 — `standard_module_of` rejects a standard module whenever `declared_module_members.contains_key(module)` is true, but that map is built during the whole-file token walk and keyed only by bare name (`src/lsp.rs:1506` `declared_module_members.insert(name, members)`), ignoring source order and frame/scope. A program that uses `files.read(...)` before a later `module files … end`, or declares a same-named module inside a function/branch that may never run, loses stdlib member navigation for those earlier/other-scope uses even though the standard module is the one in force at that point. Fix: gate the program-module shadow on declaration position and the runtime frame path (as `module_member_writes` already does), or key it by the resolved module binding rather than the name. What would settle it: a test declaring `module text` (or `files`) inside a branch/function and navigating a stdlib member use elsewhere/earlier.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 2
